  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nCurrent synoptic pattern and regional weather conditions:\nThe latest analysis and radar and satellite imagery display \nsurface high pressure stretching from James Bay to the central \nGreat Lakes beneath a dominant omega blocking ridge. Low level \nwinds continue to originate out of the dry airmass within the high\npressure system, resulting in precipitable water values hovering \naround 60 to 70 percent of normal. Upstream, shortwave energy \ntraversing the central Plains is generating light rain across \nMinnesota and Iowa where moisture is abundant. This shortwave \nenergy and deeper moisture will slowly advance toward the western \nGreat Lakes over the next 36 to 48 hours.\n\nPatchy dense fog late tonight and early Sunday:\nHigh pressure will remain in control across the region tonight,\nkeeping precipitation chances to our west. The combination of\nclear skies and light winds will promote radiational cooling \nconditions. This setup is favorable for the development of patchy \ndense ground fog over eastern and northeast Wisconsin late tonight\ninto early Sunday morning. Visibilities may be significantly \nreduced in localized areas, so travelers should remain alert for \nrapidly changing conditions.\n\nLight rain Sunday and better chances on Tuesday:\nAs deeper moisture pushes into the dry airmass on Sunday, the\nmoisture channel will become increasingly narrow. There may still\nbe sufficient saturation to support light showers and sprinkles\nover central and north-central Wisconsin during the morning (20%\nchance). High temperatures will be slightly cooler on Sunday due \nto the increased cloud cover. A more widespread period of \nprecipitation is expected from Tuesday afternoon through Tuesday \nnight, with a 20 to 40 percent chance of receiving a half inch or \nmore of rain.\n\nDry weather trends favored Wednesday through Friday:\nForecast predictability decreases considerably from Wednesday \nonward due to a large spread in guidance regarding the evolution \nof the southern stream system. Despite this uncertainty, current \ntrends support a higher potential for dry weather returning to the\narea from Wednesday through Friday.",
